WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.523
FedRAMP success depends on collaboration among many stakeholders.

00:00:03.523 --> 00:00:06.945
Cloud providers, federal agencies, engineers, administrators, security teams,

00:00:06.945 --> 00:00:11.223
compliance personnel, assessors, and leadership all contribute to the program.

00:00:11.223 --> 00:00:15.930
Cloud service providers are responsible for building and operating cloud offerings,

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.924
implementing controls, maintaining documentation, responding to findings,

00:00:18.924 --> 00:00:21.063
and supporting ongoing monitoring activities.

00:00:21.063 --> 00:00:24.914
Federal agencies are responsible for understanding their mission needs,

00:00:24.914 --> 00:00:30.048
reviewing available security information, making risk decisions for their own use cases,

00:00:30.048 --> 00:00:33.043
and monitoring the services they rely on.

00:00:33.043 --> 00:00:36.465
Assessors evaluate control implementation and provide assessment results.

00:00:36.465 --> 00:00:40.743
Security and compliance teams coordinate documentation, evidence, findings, risk discussions,

00:00:40.743 --> 00:00:42.455
and communication across stakeholders.

00:00:42.455 --> 00:00:46.733
Engineers and administrators make FedRAMP real through architecture, identity management,

00:00:46.733 --> 00:00:50.583
logging, encryption, vulnerability management, change control, automation, incident response,

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:51.867
and operational support.

00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:56.145
Leadership matters because authorization and risk management require accountable decisions.

00:00:56.145 --> 00:00:58.284
Leaders provide priorities, resources, expectations,

00:00:58.284 --> 00:01:01.279
and support for remediation and continuous improvement.

00:01:01.279 --> 00:01:08.124
The shared responsibility model is important because cloud security is not owned by one group alone.

00:01:08.124 --> 00:01:13.258
FedRAMP works best when each stakeholder understands what they own and how

00:01:13.258 --> 00:01:15.825
their work affects the security posture.

00:01:15.825 --> 00:01:17.964
Clear roles also reduce friction.

00:01:17.964 --> 00:01:23.098
When teams know who owns documentation, who fixes findings, who approves changes,

00:01:23.098 --> 00:01:28.660
and who communicates with assessors or agencies, the program becomes easier to operate.
